<document source="adrs/ADR-004-websocket-transport.md" type="adr" repo="re-cinq/the-expert-ui" relevance="1.00" tokens="496">
## Context

The Expert backend was migrated from Hapi.js + `@hapi/nes` to Express + the `hal-engine` framework. As part of that migration the `POST /chats/:id/answer` endpoint was deliberately retired: chat answers now flow over a WebSocket endpoint at `<wsUrl>/ws?sessionId=<chatId>` using HAL-engine's index-based entry protocol (`entry_upsert`, `stream_end`, `error`, `connected`, `pong`).

The Ember frontend had no WebSocket code prior to this change. The chat answer flow was strictly HTTP: optimistic local push → `POST /answer` → `chat.reload()` → diff-merge. We need a transport layer that adopts the new protocol while preserving every other piece of the UI (model attrs, REST adapter for `POST /chats` + `GET /chats/:id`, route structure, Glimmer reactivity, intro animations, progress bar, report status).

## Consequences

### Positive

- Backend and frontend share one transport contract; the FE is now a real consumer of the HAL protocol, exercising it end to end.
- Each turn requires one WS round-trip plus one `GET /chats/:id` instead of one HTTP POST + one GET. Same network footprint, lower perceived latency once we add streaming chunks in the future.
- The pure `sessionEntryToHistory` helper and `MockWebSocket` test harness are reusable for any future component that consumes the same protocol.

### Negative / accepted trade-offs

- "Question N of M" label briefly disappears between WS push and `stream_end` reload. Acceptable UX; documented.
- No retry on reconnect failure beyond one shot. Acceptable for the chat-session lifetime; would need revisiting if we add long-lived rooms or admin live-views.
- Test infrastructure now has two mock layers (Pretender for HTTP, MockWebSocket for WS). Adds a small mental tax for new tests.

